--TEST--
`transaction` hands its closure a `Core\Db\Transaction`, which has the connection's whole query surface and is still not a connection — ADR 0067 § 7, ADR 0043, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0067 § 7 replaces `beginTransaction`/`commit`/`rollBack` with one member
// that owns the scope, and what it hands the closure is a `Db\Transaction`.
// ADR 0043 has that class `implements Queryable by $connection`, so the query
// surface below is declared once — on the connection — and reached through the
// transaction without a second spelling of any of it.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

$db->transaction(fn (Core\Db\Transaction $tx): void => {
    var $rows = $tx->query("select id from accounts", []);
    var $write = $tx->execute("update accounts set owner = ? where id = ?", ["ada", 1]);
    var $written = $tx->executeMany("insert into accounts (id) values (?)", [[1], [2]]);
    $tx->rollBack("this fixture keeps nothing");
});

// And the refusal the delegation does *not* buy: sharing a surface is not
// being the same type. A helper that means "this must run inside a
// transaction" says so with a `Transaction` parameter, and one that does not
// care will say `Queryable` — neither of which a `Connection` parameter is.
class Writes {
    public static function all(Core\Db\Connection $db): void {
        $db->execute("delete from accounts", []);
    }
}

$db->transaction(fn (Core\Db\Transaction $tx): void => {
    Writes::all($tx);
});
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `Core\Db\Connection`, found `Core\Db\Transaction`
   --> case.nvs:27:17
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
